.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt DF 1
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm df
.Nd display free disk space
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl b | g | H | h | k | m | P
.Op Fl acilnT
.Op Fl t Ar type
.Op Ar file | filesystem ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
shows, for each mounted file system, its size, the space used and the space
available, how full it is, and where it is mounted.
Given a
.Ar file ,
it shows the file system holding it; given a
.Ar filesystem ,
a device, the file system mounted from it.
.Pp
Sizes are in 512-byte blocks, or in the unit
.Ev BLOCKSIZE
names.
Capacity is the share of the space ordinary users may fill that is used:
space kept back for the super-user is left out.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl a
Show all file systems (OxideBSD hides none, so this changes nothing).
.It Fl b
Sizes in 512-byte blocks.
.It Fl c
Add a line with the totals.
.It Fl g
Sizes in gigabytes (1073741824 bytes).
.It Fl H
Sizes with a unit suffix (B, K, M, G, T, P, E), in powers of 1000.
.It Fl h
Sizes with a unit suffix, in powers of 1024.
.It Fl i
Also show the inodes used and free, and the share used.
.It Fl k
Sizes in kilobytes (1024 bytes).
.It Fl l
Only local file systems.
.It Fl m
Sizes in megabytes (1048576 bytes).
.It Fl n
Use the counts the kernel has to hand rather than asking each file system,
which may be out of date but can't block.
.It Fl P
POSIX's output: its header, 512-byte blocks (1024-byte ones with
.Fl k ) ,
and capacity rounded up.
.It Fl T
Also show each file system's type.
.It Fl t Ar type Ns Op , Ns Ar type ...
Only file systems of these types.
A list beginning with
.Ql no ,
as in
.Ql noprocfs,devfs ,
means all but these.
.El
.Pp
Of
.Fl b ,
.Fl g ,
.Fl H ,
.Fl h ,
.Fl k ,
.Fl m
and
.Fl P
the last given counts, except that
.Fl k
and
.Fl P
together give POSIX's output in 1024-byte blocks.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width BLOCKSIZE
.It Ev BLOCKSIZE
The unit for sizes, such as
.Ql 1k
or
.Ql 1m ,
when none of the unit options is given.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Free space everywhere, readably:
.Dl $ df -h
.Pp
The file system holding a directory, with its type and inodes:
.Dl $ df -Ti /var/log
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr du 1 ,
.Xr getvfsstat 2 ,
.Xr statvfs 3 ,
.Xr fstab 5 ,
.Xr mount 8
.Sh STANDARDS
.Nm
conforms to
IEEE Std 1003.1-2024 (\(lqPOSIX.1\(rq).
The options but
.Fl k
and
.Fl P
are extensions, as in the BSDs.
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
command appeared in
.At v1 .
This one is written in Rust for OxideBSD.
